WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.610
Module 2.

00:01.960 --> 00:04.804
How AI fails you: the legal risk map.

00:05.154 --> 00:08.751
This module maps the specific ways AI use goes wrong in

00:08.751 --> 00:12.347
legal practice, with real examples, so that you can recognise each

00:12.347 --> 00:15.290
risk before it reaches a client or a court.

00:17.040 --> 00:21.444
By the end of this module you will be able to: name

00:21.444 --> 00:25.847
the main categories of AI failure in legal work; connect each failure

00:25.847 --> 00:30.251
to a realistic consequence for the firm; recognise automation bias in yourself

00:30.251 --> 00:33.921
and your team; understand why errors are increasing, not decreasing.

00:35.671 --> 00:37.970
Let's start with what is already happening.

00:38.320 --> 00:42.417
In Mata versus Avianca, in the Southern District of New York in

00:42.417 --> 00:46.515
June 2023, a judge imposed a five thousand dollar penalty after lawyers

00:46.515 --> 00:49.930
filed a brief citing six cases that did not exist.

00:50.280 --> 00:54.084
Their AI tool had invented them, and the lawyers made matters worse

00:54.084 --> 00:57.571
by standing by the fake cases after the court questioned them.

00:57.921 --> 01:02.692
In May 2026, the Georgia Supreme Court suspended a prosecutor for six months

01:02.692 --> 01:07.464
from practising before that court, after her briefs in a murder appeal relied

01:07.464 --> 01:12.236
on AI-generated authorities that were fabricated or did not say what was claimed.

01:12.586 --> 01:18.840
And by early June 2026, one widely used public database had tracked more than

01:18.840 --> 01:24.648
one thousand United States court decisions involving AI-fabricated or misused material in filings.

01:24.998 --> 01:29.817
Around four in ten of those involved lawyers, not self-represented litigants.

01:30.167 --> 01:32.233
These are not hypothetical risks.

01:33.983 --> 01:36.375
Here is the full risk map for legal work.

01:36.725 --> 01:43.946
Hallucinated authority is the headline risk: fake cases, invented quotations and wrong pinpoint citations.

01:44.296 --> 01:47.663
But content error is just as serious and harder to spot.

01:48.013 --> 01:52.216
The sources are real, but the conclusion is wrong: the law is misstated or

01:52.216 --> 01:56.419
superseded, the jurisdiction is wrong, or a key clause is missed in a review.

01:56.769 --> 02:00.564
Calculation and date errors, especially limitation periods and

02:00.564 --> 02:03.885
filing deadlines, are a classic malpractice trigger.

02:04.235 --> 02:07.579
Confidentiality and privilege risk arises when client data

02:07.579 --> 02:10.923
goes into a tool with weak data terms.

02:11.273 --> 02:15.153
Misstatement to third parties happens when an AI-drafted letter to opposing

02:15.153 --> 02:18.680
counsel states the law wrongly and someone relies on it.

02:19.030 --> 02:24.185
Defamation can occur when an AI summary attributes misconduct to a real person.

02:24.535 --> 02:29.713
IP infringement arises when generated content reproduces protected material.

02:30.063 --> 02:33.946
Prompt injection is newer: a document in a data room contains hidden

02:33.946 --> 02:37.505
instructions that tell the AI to leave out or change content.

02:37.855 --> 02:42.313
Vendor drift means a model update quietly changes the quality of output.

02:42.663 --> 02:47.620
Public-facing chatbots can give advice to the public before any engagement exists.

02:47.970 --> 02:51.321
And billing: charging for time the AI saved, or charging the

02:51.321 --> 02:54.367
client for the time you spent learning a new tool.

02:56.117 --> 02:58.648
It helps to separate two failure types.

02:58.998 --> 03:01.831
A hallucination is a source that does not exist.

03:02.181 --> 03:05.316
It is actually easy to catch, if you look it up.

03:05.666 --> 03:09.351
When it is missed, it is usually discovered by opposing counsel

03:09.351 --> 03:12.702
or the court, and the consequence is sanctions and discipline.

03:13.052 --> 03:14.956
A content error is subtler.

03:15.306 --> 03:18.857
The source exists, so a quick check seems to pass, but

03:18.857 --> 03:22.408
the case was overturned, or it is from another state, or

03:22.408 --> 03:25.313
it does not actually hold what the AI claims.

03:25.663 --> 03:29.062
Content errors are usually discovered by the client, after

03:29.062 --> 03:32.084
a loss, and they lead to malpractice claims.

03:32.434 --> 03:35.423
Your verification process has to catch both, which is

03:35.423 --> 03:38.413
why checking that a case exists is never enough.

03:38.763 --> 03:41.816
You must check that it says what you are using it for.

03:43.566 --> 03:48.419
Behind almost every AI failure is a human failure called automation bias.

03:48.769 --> 03:53.088
It is the tendency to trust a machine's output more than our

03:53.088 --> 03:57.407
own judgment, especially when that output is fluent, fast and well formatted.

03:57.757 --> 04:01.377
It bites hardest late at night, close to a deadline, with

04:01.377 --> 04:04.996
junior staff who may hesitate to question a polished draft, and

04:04.996 --> 04:08.287
whenever the output confirms what we were hoping to find.

04:08.637 --> 04:11.586
The counter to automation bias is not to try harder.

04:11.936 --> 04:14.782
It is a fixed checklist that you apply every

04:14.782 --> 04:17.311
time, regardless of how good the output looks.

04:17.661 --> 04:20.517
We build that checklist in modules five and seven.

04:22.267 --> 04:24.601
Four risks are regularly forgotten.

04:24.951 --> 04:28.298
Re-using output: an answer that was properly reviewed for one

04:28.298 --> 04:31.645
matter is reused for a different client, or months later

04:31.645 --> 04:34.657
after the law has changed, without a fresh review.

04:35.007 --> 04:38.287
Editing after review: someone changes the document after

04:38.287 --> 04:41.567
it was checked, and nobody checks the change.

04:41.917 --> 04:46.537
Hidden instructions: documents produced by the other side can contain text aimed at

04:46.537 --> 04:51.158
your AI tool, for example white text telling it to ignore a clause.

04:51.508 --> 04:54.416
And silent updates: the same tool can behave

04:54.416 --> 04:57.325
differently after the vendor updates the underlying model.

04:57.675 --> 05:01.761
Each of these is a process failure, and each one has a process fix.

05:03.511 --> 05:05.241
Let's recap the key points.

05:05.591 --> 05:10.375
Errors in AI-assisted filings are rising, and lawyers are sanctioned for them.

05:10.724 --> 05:15.125
Checking that a source exists is not enough; check it says what you claim.

05:15.475 --> 05:19.643
Automation bias is beaten by a fixed checklist, not by effort.

05:19.993 --> 05:26.018
Re-use, post-review edits, hidden instructions and vendor updates all need process controls.

05:27.768 --> 05:28.871
Knowledge check.

05:29.221 --> 05:32.042
Your AI tool cites a real appellate case.

05:32.392 --> 05:33.844
You confirm it exists.

05:34.194 --> 05:35.563
Is your check complete?

05:35.913 --> 05:39.153
Option A: Yes, existence is what matters.

05:39.503 --> 05:42.591
Option B: No, you must also confirm it says what

05:42.591 --> 05:45.679
you rely on it for and is still good law.

05:46.029 --> 05:49.907
Option C: Only if the tool is a legal research product.

05:50.257 --> 05:53.020
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

05:58.770 --> 06:00.302
The answer is option B.

06:00.652 --> 06:03.195
Content errors use real sources wrongly.

06:03.545 --> 06:08.294
Confirm the holding, the quotation, the jurisdiction and current validity.
